          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>In the clinical case presented, which finding is the hallmark early sign of infa</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>In the clinical case presented, which finding is the hallmark early sign of infantile hemiplegia?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bilateral lower limb spasticity</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Bilateral lower limb spasticity suggests diplegic cerebral palsy, not hemiplegia. Infantile hemiplegia is characterised by UNILATERAL upper motor neurone signs affecting one side of the body.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Generalised hypotonia with head lag</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Generalised hypotonia points toward a floppy infant evaluation. Infantile hemiplegia shows INCREASED tone (spasticity) on the affected side, not generalised floppiness.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Asymmetric hand preference before 12 months of age</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. Establishing hand dominance before 12 months is abnormal — normal hand preference develops after 18 months. Early asymmetric use indicates unilateral UMN involvement, the hallmark of infantile hemiplegia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Absent deep tendon reflexes on the affected side</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Absent reflexes suggest a lower motor neurone (peripheral) lesion. In infantile hemiplegia, deep tendon reflexes are BRISK (exaggerated) because the upper motor neurone pathway is damaged.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "The hallmark is asymmetric hand preference before 12 months of age. Normal hand dominance develops after 18 months; earlier preference signals unilateral upper motor neurone pathology."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. Asymmetric hand preference before 12 months — when normal hand dominance has not yet developed — is the hallmark early warning sign of infantile hemiplegia."
                }
              ],
