          "buttonOnMobile": false,
          "label": "<p>What does the term 'non-progressive' in the definition of cerebral palsy specifi</p>",
          "x": 30,
          "y": 30,
          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>What does the term 'non-progressive' in the definition of cerebral palsy specifically refer to?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>The child's motor abilities will neither improve nor worsen over time</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Motor abilities CAN improve with rehabilitation, and the clinical picture may worsen as contractures develop and motor demands increase. 'Non-progressive' refers to the brain lesion, not the clinical course.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The underlying brain lesion does not advance, even though clinical manifestations may change as the child grows</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. The narration emphasises that the brain lesion itself is static, but contractures, increased motor demands, and emerging cognitive and behavioural comorbidities can alter the clinical picture over time.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The condition does not require long-term management since it will not progress</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>CP requires lifelong multidisciplinary management. 'Non-progressive' describes the brain pathology, not the need for treatment — physiotherapy, orthopaedic care, and comorbidity management remain essential.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>The clinical features present at birth remain the sole manifestations throughout life</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Clinical features evolve substantially with age — new contractures form, spasticity may increase with growth, and cognitive, sensory, or behavioural comorbidities can become apparent later in childhood.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "'Non-progressive' means the underlying brain lesion does not advance. However, clinical manifestations evolve as the child grows — contractures develop, motor demands increase, and comorbidities emerge."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Exactly. The brain lesion is static but the child's clinical picture is dynamic — a crucial distinction for counselling families and planning long-term management."
                }
              ],
