          "width": 40,
          "height": 25,
          "libraryTitle": "Multiple Choice",
          "action": {
            "library": "H5P.MultiChoice 1.16",
            "params": {
              "question": "<p>A 14-month-old child is clinically diagnosed with infantile hemiplegia. Which investigation specifically addresses the need to establish the underlying aetiology?</p>",
              "answers": [
                {
                  "text": "<div>MRI of the brain</div>",
                  "correct": true,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Correct. MRI identifies the structural, vascular, or malformative brain lesion causing the hemiplegia — fulfilling the first investigative purpose of aetiological diagnosis for specific counselling and management.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Electroencephalography (EEG)</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>EEG characterises seizure type to guide antiepileptic drug selection — the second of the four investigative purposes. It does not establish the structural aetiology of the hemiplegia.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Bayley Scales of Infant Development</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Developmental assessment quantifies impairments across cognitive, language, and behavioural domains — the third investigative purpose, distinct from aetiological diagnosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                },
                {
                  "text": "<div>Radiograph of the hips</div>",
                  "correct": false,
                  "tipsAndFeedback": {
                    "tip": "",
                    "chosenFeedback": "<div>Hip radiography screens for orthopaedic complications and monitors hip development — the fourth investigative purpose, not aetiological diagnosis.</div>",
                    "notChosenFeedback": ""
                  }
                }
              ],
              "overallFeedback": [
                {
                  "from": 0,
                  "to": 50,
                  "feedback": "MRI of the brain establishes the aetiology. The narration outlined four distinct investigative purposes: aetiology (MRI), epilepsy characterisation (EEG), developmental assessment, and orthopaedic screening — a structured approach rather than a blanket battery of tests."
                },
                {
                  "from": 51,
                  "to": 100,
                  "feedback": "Well done. MRI is the key investigation for aetiological diagnosis — it can reveal cortical dysplasia, prenatal infarction, periventricular leucomalacia, or other structural lesions that guide specific counselling."
                }
              ],
